// `copy`, `give` and `truncate` name a BINDING — a local, a parameter, a field, a member, a pattern — exactly as `type`,
// `slot` and `file` do, and like them they name no type. The message names the word and the rule.
type value give { public int32 x; }
fn uint8 main() { return 0; }
// tree-sitter refuses it too (KR-29): a type, an enum member, a function and a type parameter take the `declared` reserved
// set, the six contextual words included, so this file is in tree-sitter-kama/test/parse-errors.txt.
